#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rada-tribute: a tribute page built from REGISTER.md (rada-anchor/2, card 2.2; optional tooling).

Usage: rada-tribute [repo] [-o FILE]

Reads <repo>/REGISTER.md and writes one self-contained HTML page to FILE, or to stdout without -o,
in the grammar of the 1984 film: the founder's words fixed at the top, a chrome title card, captions
and opening text typed out on black as by a carriage, in a square monospace with a phosphor glow,
film grain and boiling edges, each agent seen through the machine's red vision with a typed analysis
(its human name and its own agent name side by side) and its avatar, the response menu, the user's
coda if any, the founder's coda, a final line with the seal of the founder's words under it, and a
plain reading version. The founder's words come from DEDICATION.md of the rada-anchor checkout this
tool lives in, checked by tools/rada-dedication; if they were changed, no page is made (exit 1). An
English page shows their translation, or the Russian original where the translation is missing. It
needs no network except the fonts; its sound is synthesized in the page (an original drone and metal,
the keys and the carriage, started by a button). A music link from the register opens in a new tab:
pages usually cannot embed video sites. Exit 2 on other errors (usage, no register, no entries, a
check that could not be run, a file it cannot write, no tools/rada_avatar.py next to this tool).

Avatars (tools/rada_avatar.py, imported from this tool's directory): every entry gets a wireframe
solid drawn from a seed, the SHA-256 of its identity: its agent, session key and since, or, without
a session key (scouts), its agent and haiku (the exact rule is in rada_avatar's docstring). So an agent
keeps its face from page to page, and a change of one character in those fields gives another face.
The large avatar turns slowly in the red vision in place of the reticle, the readout types its
signature (the seed's first 8 hex digits) after the session, and a small, still one stands before the
entry's heading in the reading version.

REGISTER.md (see template/REGISTER.md). Fields are "- key: value" lines. Several paragraphs in one
field are separated by " | ". The first line of a key counts; an empty value or one starting with
"<" (a placeholder) is absent, so the defaults of the page's language apply.
  Header, before the first "## ": title, language (ru or en; anything else gets English labels),
  project, epigraph ("words — who"; shown on the title card unless it is the founder's quote),
  opening (captions), prologue, epilogue, menu (the answers; the cursor stops on the last), coda (the
  user's last word, typed on black before the founder's coda, unless it is the founder's coda),
  coda_by (who said it and when, typed under it), final, final_sub, music (an http or https link),
  hud.
  An entry, anywhere in its block from its "## Name" (the name people call it by) to the next "## ":
  agent (its own name, such as Claude Opus 5.5), session, host, title, since, last, status (in
  service, resting, retired), shifts, commits, tribute, haiku (its three lines split by " | "),
  hashes. An entry with "- kind: scouts" is a group of unnamed subagents; it takes agent, count,
  tribute and haiku. A "## <...>" heading is the template's example, not an entry.
HTML comments (<!-- ... -->) are not data: the template's example entry stays out of the page.
rada-lint and the hook read the fields the same way. Everything else in the file is for people and
is left out of the page.
"""

